      "text": "Los Glaciares and the Perito Moreno Glacier"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:2e780f63f4172747",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "e285dd537cd4af5aeec6154b077d0be7044ec5645a4413037a1496d076567781",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261107-eab366ae-e542-49a4-8e67-bf54cf3f8a2d",
        "model": "ChatGPT"
      },
      "text": "Breakfast at the hotel. The excursion to the Perito Moreno Glacier in Los Glaciares National Park takes around **6–8 hours**.\n\nIce fields cover about 40% of the park. The park has 47 major glaciers, 13 of which flow east and feed lakes Viedma and Argentino.\n\nPerito Moreno lies about **80 km from El Calafate** and is known for its constant movement. The glacier face is about **60 m** high. From time to time, enormous chunks of ice break off and fall into the Brazo Rico arm of the lake or the Témpanos Channel.\n\nAs it advances towards the Magallanes Peninsula, the glacier periodically blocks off part of the lake. The water level in Brazo Rico can rise about **35 m** above average. The water then gradually breaks through the ice dam. The cycle takes around **4–6 years**.\n\nViewing platforms and walkways let you see the glacier from different heights. During the day, you can hear the cracks and crashes of falling ice."
    },
